CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In a state, a party wins 90 of 200 seats in an assembly election and cannot form the government by itself. Several smaller parties then join it, and together they cross the halfway mark to form a government. The remaining parties sit on the opposite benches and criticise the government's policies. "
   r"(i) What is the term for such a government formed by several parties together? (ii) How many seats are needed for a majority in a 200-seat assembly? (iii) What role do the parties on the opposite benches play? (iv) Why is a strong opposition useful in a democracy?",
   r"(i) A coalition government (1). (ii) 101 seats (1). (iii) They are the opposition (1). (iv) It criticises the government's failures, offers alternatives and keeps it accountable, so the government is less likely to misuse power (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> A citizen wants to know why voters often find little difference between parties. She notes problems like parties not holding internal elections, leaders' children being preferred, the use of money and muscle power in elections and the lack of meaningful choice. The Supreme Court and the Election Commission have taken steps to deal with some of these. "
   r"(i) Name any one challenge faced by political parties. (ii) What is meant by dynastic succession in parties? (iii) Which institution has made it compulsory for candidates to file affidavits about property and criminal cases? (iv) Suggest any one way of reforming political parties.",
   r"(i) Any one: lack of internal democracy, dynastic succession, money and muscle power, lack of meaningful choice (1). (ii) Top positions go to relatives of party leaders (1). (iii) The Supreme Court (1). (iv) Any one: anti-defection law, regulation of party finances, compulsory organisational elections, state funding of elections; with reason (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In country A there is only one party, which controls all governments. In country B two parties generally win power alternately. In country C many parties compete and coalitions form governments. A student of political science is asked to classify the party systems and to name an example of each. "
   r"(i) What is the system in country A called? (ii) Name a country with a two-party system. (iii) Which system does India have? (iv) Why is the one-party system not considered democratic?",
   r"(i) One-party system (1). (ii) The USA or the UK (1). (iii) Multi-party system (1). (iv) It does not allow free competition, as other parties cannot contest and people have no real choice, so the government is not accountable (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

